/*****************************************************************************
    Tab stops in a style (idea 1; Clay, forwarded by Tony 2026-11-14, SEQ 661; opinion only, nothing built or ruled)
*****************************************************************************/
THE IDEA: a style may set tab stops; a tab in typeset text skips to the next stop. Stops are measured from the field's left text edge, a bare number points, N% of the field's width. Past a stop, the next one; past the last, one space. Left stops first. A line holding a tab is not justified. Tabs inherit as text properties do. Short pieces on one line only: cells that wrap are a table (Topics/features 34). First customer: the Reference in a PDF.
CLOD'S READING (from the code; not measured): the floor does not fight it. drawSpans hands Core Text one string a paragraph, and Core Text has tab stops of its own (a paragraph setting): left, right, centre and decimal, and "past this stop, the next" is its own behaviour. Justify is decided line by line in the floor, so "not justified when the line holds a tab" is one test there.
    WHAT FIGHTS, all in kant's typeset: (1) its grammar reads a tab as white space (tsSp is tab, return or space) and a run of white space is one gap, so a tab is a space before the floor sees it; a tab needs a unit of its own and a span that says so. (2) one line end is white space too; only two end a paragraph. Rows arriving one a line would run together: a line end must close the row when tabs are set, or each row be its own paragraph.
    NOT COVERED BY THE IDEA: past the last stop Core Text goes on at a default interval, not one space (to be measured); whether a tab survives a kant literal, a (...#) value and getFile is not measured; tabs would be a new form attribute, so it needs a definition in FORMs and a fact (the first in the typeset group);
    the Reference is 28 rows and 5 headings, more than one page of IBkant at 14 points, and a passage too long is cut at its field's foot today (multi-page forms are outside the show dispatch); the walk prints to the terminal, so it must first build its rows as one text a field can hold.

